borrowed value 'c' is captured by the lambda that a `_` placeholder makes of its call, which is not yet supported (higher-order linearity is deferred; the closure may run after the owner releases it); read 'c' before the call and capture the result instead; if the `_` stands for the call's data argument, write the call without it, so a `local` slot inside it can lend 'c'; or, if the callee is your own and does not keep this closure past the call, declare that parameter `local`, as the prelude's concrete `list_`, `vec_`, `range_` and `result_` functions and the `Foldable` functions do; the class methods `map`, `filter`, `and_then` and `traverse` do not
